;; Docs for std/rx.bjo, read through (:doc #:file "rx.bjodoc"). The texts are
;; samizdat markup: code in prose is @code{...}.

(:doc #:module
  (summary "Regular expressions written as s-expressions, checked and compiled at compile time.")
  (see rx-match? rx-search rx-compile RxMatch)
  (example """
    (import (std rx))

    (def phone #rx(seq :bos
                       (=> :area (= 3 :ascii-digit))   ; three digits, named :area
                       (lit "-")
                       (=> :line (= 4 :ascii-digit))   ; four digits, named :line
                       :eos))

    (rx-match? phone "555-1234")          ; #t
    (rx-search #rx((+ :digit)) "a42b")    ; (Some <match on "42">)
    """)
  (reference """
    A pattern is written @code{#rx(...)} in a notation like Scheme's SRE,
    not as a string such as @code{"[a-z]+"}, so nothing is ever escaped
    twice. It is read, checked and compiled to a .NET pattern at compile
    time, and a bad pattern is a compile error pointing at the sub-form that
    is wrong. The pattern language is described with @code{#rx}.

    @strong{Using a pattern.} A @code{#rx(...)} is an @code{Rx}, a compiled
    regular expression. Compiling is cached by pattern, so evaluating the
    same @code{#rx(...)} again does not build a second .NET regex.
    @code{rx-match?} and @code{rx-match} match the
    whole string; @code{rx-search?} and @code{rx-search} find the leftmost
    match anywhere in it; @code{rx-matches} finds them all. A match is an
    @code{RxMatch}, read with @code{rx-text}, @code{rx-start},
    @code{rx-end} and the @code{rx-group} family. @code{rx-replace},
    @code{rx-replace-with} and @code{rx-split} rewrite a string. In a
    @code{match}, the patterns @code{Rx} and @code{RxIn} do the same as
    @code{rx-match} and @code{rx-search}.

    @strong{The engine.} The matching is
    @code{System.Text.RegularExpressions} in its non-backtracking mode,
    which matches in time linear in the length of the string, and the
    options are fixed: @code{NonBacktracking}, @code{ExplicitCapture},
    @code{CultureInvariant}. That mode refuses backreferences, lookarounds,
    atomic groups and conditionals, the constructs that would tie Bjolang to
    a backtracking engine for good. So these are not supported, on purpose:

    @itemlist{
      @item{Lookarounds, backreferences and conditionals.}
      @item{Complementing a whole pattern. A character set can be
        complemented: @code{(~ :digit)}.}
      @item{Case-insensitive matching, which would depend on the runtime's
        culture tables.}
      @item{Patterns built at run time. A pattern is built at compile time,
        so @code{,x} inside @code{#rx(...)} is an error: every pattern is
        known statically.}
    }

    @strong{Unicode.} The alphabet is the Unicode scalar, as it is for
    @code{char} and the string cursors, but .NET's engine works in UTF-16
    code units. So @code{:any} and every complemented set are emitted as an
    alternation that takes a surrogate pair as one unit, and a match never
    begins or ends inside a pair: an emoji is not split in half.

    .NET's character classes cannot name a scalar above U+FFFF, so a
    character set cannot hold one: @code{(in "😀")} is a compile error.
    Match it as a literal, @code{"😀"} or @code{(lit "😀")}, instead.

    The emitted pattern never uses @code{.}, @code{^}, @code{$},
    @code{\d}, @code{\w}, @code{\s} or @code{\p{...}}, whose meanings .NET
    decides and which move when the runtime's Unicode tables are updated.
    The named sets are written out as explicit ranges from tables in
    @code{rx.bjo}, generated from .NET's @code{CharUnicodeInfo} so that
    @code{:alpha} agrees with @code{char-alphabetic?}. A compiled program
    means the same thing on every runtime.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The types
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

;; `Rx` is also the name of the pattern macro below, which is what (:doc Rx)
;; documents; the type is described in rx-compile's doc and the module's.

(:doc RxMatch
  (summary "One match: the string it was found in, where, and its groups.")
  (see rx-text rx-start rx-group rx-group/n))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; The pattern language
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc rx #:reader
  (summary "A regular expression, checked and compiled at compile time.")
  (form (rx pattern))
  (arg pattern "The pattern, in the notation below.")
  (see rx-compile rx-match rx-search)
  (example """
    #rx(seq :bos (+ :digit) (lit "-") ($ (** 4 4 :digit)) :eos)
    #rx(:digit)              ; one argument is the pattern itself
    #rx((+ :digit))
    #rx(+ :digit)            ; and the same as #rx((+ :digit))
    """)
  (reference """
    It expands to @code{(rx-compile "<pattern>" "<names>")}, with the .NET
    pattern and the group names worked out at compile time. Its value is an
    @code{Rx}. @code{#rx(seq a b)} reads as @code{(#rx seq a b)}, so a form
    may be written with or without its own parentheses.

    @strong{Two spelling rules.} Anything that takes no argument is a
    keyword: @code{:bos}, @code{:any}, @code{:digit}. Anything that takes
    arguments is a form headed by a symbol: @code{(seq ...)}, @code{(+ ...)},
    @code{(in ...)}. A string or a character is itself: a literal sequence
    in pattern position, the set of its characters inside a set.

    @read-table{
    | Pattern                                      | Matches                                                          |
    |----------------------------------------------+------------------------------------------------------------------|
    | @code{"text"}, @code{#\c}                    | Exactly that text or character.                                  |
    | @code{(lit "text")}                          | Exactly that text. It takes one string or character.             |
    | @code{(seq a b ...)}                         | @code{a}, then @code{b}, and so on.                              |
    | @code{(or a b ...)}                          | @code{a} or @code{b} or ...                                      |
    | @code{(* r)}, @code{(+ r)}, @code{(? r)}     | Zero or more, one or more, or zero or one of @code{r}.           |
    | @code{(*? r)}, @code{(+? r)}, @code{(?? r)}  | The same, lazily: as few as will do.                             |
    | @code{(** least most r)}                     | @code{r}, from @code{least} to @code{most} times.                |
    | @code{(= n r)}, @code{(>= n r)}              | @code{r} exactly @code{n} times, or at least @code{n} times.     |
    | @code{($ r ...)}                             | @code{r}, captured in an unnamed group.                          |
    | @code{(=> :name r ...)}                      | @code{r}, captured in a group named by the keyword.              |
    | @code{:bos}, @code{:eos}                     | The beginning and the end of the string.                         |
    | @code{:any}                                  | Any one character, newlines included.                            |
    | @code{:nonl}                                 | Any one character but U+000A, the only line break it knows.      |
    | a named set, or a set form                   | One character of the set.                                        |
    }

    A repetition, @code{$} or @code{=>} given several patterns takes them
    in sequence: @code{(+ "a" "b")} is @code{(+ (seq "a" "b"))}. Counts are
    whole numbers. Groups are numbered from zero in the order they open.

    @strong{Character sets} match one character. They are worked out at
    compile time, and a set that can match nothing is an error.

    @read-table{
    | Set                          | Holds                                                                      |
    |------------------------------+----------------------------------------------------------------------------|
    | @code{#\c}, @code{"abc"}     | Those characters.                                                          |
    | @code{(in a b ...)}          | Every character in any of the sets; @code{(or a b ...)} inside a set is the same. |
    | @code{(/ lo hi ...)}         | The ranges from @code{lo} to @code{hi}, the endpoints taken in pairs.      |
    | @code{(~ a b ...)}           | Every character in none of the sets.                                       |
    | @code{(- a b ...)}           | The characters of @code{a} that are in none of the others.                 |
    | @code{(& a b ...)}           | The characters in all of the sets.                                         |
    }

    @code{(/ #\a #\z #\0 #\9)} and @code{(/ "az09")} are the same set,
    the lowercase letters and the digits: the endpoints are taken in pairs
    however they are written. A range runs upwards. A set member or a range
    endpoint is a character in U+0000 to U+FFFF; above that, match a
    literal instead.

    @strong{The named sets} are keywords. Where two names are given they are
    the same set.

    @read-table{
    | Name                                    | Holds                                                         |
    |-----------------------------------------+---------------------------------------------------------------|
    | @code{:alpha}, @code{:alphabetic}       | Letters, in all of Unicode.                                   |
    | @code{:digit}, @code{:numeric}          | Every Unicode decimal digit, as @code{char-numeric?} answers. |
    | @code{:alnum}, @code{:alphanumeric}     | Letters and decimal digits.                                   |
    | @code{:upper}, @code{:upper-case}       | Uppercase letters.                                            |
    | @code{:lower}, @code{:lower-case}       | Lowercase letters.                                            |
    | @code{:space}, @code{:whitespace}       | Whitespace: U+0009 to U+000D, the space, and Unicode's others. |
    | @code{:word}                            | Letters, digits, marks and connector punctuation such as @code{_}. |
    | @code{:control}                         | U+0000 to U+001F and U+007F to U+009F.                        |
    | @code{:hex-digit}                       | @code{0} to @code{9}, @code{A} to @code{F} and @code{a} to @code{f}. |
    | @code{:ascii}                           | U+0000 to U+007F.                                             |
    | @code{:ascii-digit}                     | @code{0} to @code{9}.                                         |
    | @code{:ascii-alpha}                     | @code{A} to @code{Z} and @code{a} to @code{z}.                |
    | @code{:ascii-alnum}                     | The ASCII letters and digits.                                 |
    | @code{:any}                             | Every character.                                              |
    | @code{:nonl}                            | Every character but U+000A.                                   |
    }

    @strong{Mind :digit.} It holds every Unicode decimal digit, such as ٩
    and ०, not only @code{0} to @code{9}. A pattern for the ASCII digits
    wants @code{:ascii-digit}, and the same goes for @code{:alpha} and
    @code{:alnum}.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Matching
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc rx-match
  (summary "Matches a pattern against the whole string.")
  (arg rx "The pattern.")
  (arg s "The string.")
  (returns "The match, anchored at both ends, or None.")
  (see rx-match? rx-search Rx)
  (example """
    (match (rx-match phone "555-1234")
      ((Some m) (rx-group m :area))   ; (Some "555")
      (None None))
    """))

(:doc rx-match?
  (summary "Whether a pattern matches the whole string.")
  (arg rx "The pattern.")
  (arg s "The string.")
  (returns "#t if the whole of s matches.")
  (see rx-match rx-search?)
  (example """
    (rx-match? #rx((+ :ascii-digit)) "123")    ; #t
    (rx-match? #rx((+ :ascii-digit)) "123a")   ; #f
    """))

(:doc rx-search
  (summary "The leftmost match anywhere in the string.")
  (arg rx "The pattern.")
  (arg s "The string.")
  (returns "The leftmost match, or None.")
  (see rx-search? rx-match rx-matches RxIn)
  (example """
    (rx-search #rx((+ :digit)) "a42b")   ; (Some <match on "42">)
    """))

(:doc rx-search?
  (summary "Whether a pattern matches anywhere in the string.")
  (arg rx "The pattern.")
  (arg s "The string.")
  (returns "#t if some part of s matches.")
  (see rx-search rx-match?))

(:doc rx-matches
  (summary "Every non-overlapping match, left to right.")
  (arg rx "The pattern.")
  (arg s "The string.")
  (returns "The matches, in order; empty if there are none.")
  (see rx-search rx-split)
  (example """
    (vec-map rx-text (rx-matches #rx((+ :digit)) "a1b22c333"))   ; ["1" "22" "333"]
    """)
  (reference """
    Each search starts where the last match ended. After an empty match it
    starts one character on, a whole surrogate pair if that is what is
    there, so it is never inside one.
    """))

(:doc rx-replace
  (summary "The string with every match replaced by a fixed text.")
  (arg rx "The pattern.")
  (arg s "The string.")
  (arg replacement "The text to put in place of each match, taken literally.")
  (returns "The new string.")
  (see rx-replace-with)
  (example """
    (rx-replace #rx((+ :space)) "a  b   c" " ")   ; "a b c"
    """)
  (reference """
    There is no @code{$1} substitution syntax: that would be a second
    pattern language, arriving without being decided on. Use
    @code{rx-replace-with} to build the replacement from the match.
    """))

(:doc rx-replace-with
  (summary "The string with every match replaced by what a function makes of it.")
  (arg rx "The pattern.")
  (arg s "The string.")
  (arg f "Given each match, answers its replacement.")
  (returns "The new string.")
  (see rx-replace)
  (example """
    (rx-replace-with #rx(($ (+ :ascii-digit)))
                     "3 and 4"
                     (fun (m) (str "<" (rx-text m) ">")))   ; "<3> and <4>"
    """))

(:doc rx-split
  (summary "The pieces of the string between the matches.")
  (arg rx "The separator pattern.")
  (arg s "The string.")
  (returns "The pieces, in order. The empty pieces before a leading and after a trailing separator are kept; with no match, the whole string.")
  (see rx-matches)
  (example """
    (rx-split #rx(seq "," (* :space)) "a, b,c")   ; ["a" "b" "c"]
    """)
  (reference """
    An empty match is not a separator, so a pattern that can match nothing
    does not split between every character.
    """))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; Reading a match
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc rx-text
  (summary "The text a match matched.")
  (arg m "The match.")
  (returns "The matched part of the string.")
  (see rx-start rx-group))

(:doc rx-input
  (summary "The string a match was found in.")
  (arg m "The match.")
  (returns "The whole string that was searched, which its cursors index.")
  (see rx-start rx-end))

(:doc rx-start
  (summary "Where a match begins.")
  (arg m "The match.")
  (returns "A cursor into rx-input, on the match's first character.")
  (raises System.InvalidOperationException "When the position falls inside a surrogate pair, which only a pattern written by hand for rx-compile can bring about.")
  (see rx-end rx-input))

(:doc rx-end
  (summary "Where a match ends.")
  (arg m "The match.")
  (returns "A cursor into rx-input, just after the match's last character.")
  (raises System.InvalidOperationException "When the position falls inside a surrogate pair, which only a pattern written by hand for rx-compile can bring about.")
  (see rx-start rx-input))

(:doc rx-group
  (summary "The text a named group captured.")
  (arg m "The match.")
  (arg name "The keyword the group was named with, in @code{(=> :name ...)}.")
  (returns "The captured text, or None if the group took no part in the match. An unknown name is None too.")
  (see rx-group/n rx-group-start rx-group-end)
  (example """
    (rx-group m :area)   ; (Some "555")
    """))

(:doc rx-group-start
  (summary "Where a named group's capture begins.")
  (arg m "The match.")
  (arg name "The keyword the group was named with.")
  (returns "A cursor into rx-input, or None if the group took no part in the match or there is no such group.")
  (raises System.InvalidOperationException "When the position falls inside a surrogate pair, which only a pattern written by hand for rx-compile can bring about.")
  (see rx-group rx-group-end))

(:doc rx-group-end
  (summary "Where a named group's capture ends.")
  (arg m "The match.")
  (arg name "The keyword the group was named with.")
  (returns "A cursor into rx-input just after the capture, or None if the group took no part in the match or there is no such group.")
  (raises System.InvalidOperationException "When the position falls inside a surrogate pair, which only a pattern written by hand for rx-compile can bring about.")
  (see rx-group rx-group-start))

(:doc rx-group/n
  (summary "The text a group captured, by its number.")
  (arg m "The match.")
  (arg n "The group's number, counted from zero in the order the groups open. Named and unnamed groups are counted alike.")
  (returns "The captured text, or None if the group took no part in the match or there is no such group.")
  (see rx-group rx-group-count)
  (example """
    (match (rx-search #rx(seq ($ (+ :ascii-alpha)) "=" ($ (+ :ascii-digit))) "x=42")
      ((Some m) (rx-group/n m 1))   ; (Some "42")
      (None None))
    """))

(:doc rx-group-count
  (summary "How many groups the pattern has.")
  (arg m "The match.")
  (returns "The number of groups, named and unnamed, whether or not they took part in this match.")
  (see rx-group/n))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; What #rx(...) expands into
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc rx-compile
  (summary "Compiles a .NET pattern into an Rx: what #rx(...) expands into.")
  (arg pattern "The .NET pattern, as @code{#rx} writes it.")
  (arg names "The Bjolang name of each group in order, separated by commas. An unnamed group has an empty name.")
  (returns "The compiled regular expression.")
  (raises System.ArgumentException "When pattern is not a valid .NET pattern.")
  (raises System.NotSupportedException "When pattern uses a construct the non-backtracking engine refuses, such as a backreference or a lookaround.")
  (see rx-pattern)
  (reference """
    An @code{Rx} is a compiled regular expression. The options are fixed:
    @code{NonBacktracking}, @code{ExplicitCapture} and
    @code{CultureInvariant}. Compiling is cached by the pattern and the
    names, so the same pattern compiled twice is one regex.

    It is there for @code{#rx}, which checks what it emits. A pattern passed
    here by hand is not checked, and is not held to the promises the module
    makes: it may use @code{.}, @code{\d} and the rest, and may let a match
    boundary fall inside a surrogate pair. Commas cannot occur in a keyword,
    so they cannot occur in a name.
    """))

(:doc rx-pattern
  (summary "The .NET pattern an Rx was compiled from.")
  (arg rx "The compiled regular expression.")
  (returns "The pattern string, so that a test can check what was emitted and not only what it matches.")
  (see rx-compile))

;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
;; In a match
;; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

(:doc Rx
  (summary "A match pattern: matches a regular expression against the whole string.")
  (form (Rx regex pattern))
  (arg regex "The regular expression: an @code{Rx}, such as a @code{#rx(...)} or a name bound to one.")
  (arg pattern "Matched against the @code{(Option RxMatch)} that @code{rx-match} answers.")
  (see RxIn rx-match)
  (example """
    (match line
      ((Rx phone (Some m)) (str "phone: " (option-ref-or (rx-group m :area) "?")))
      ((RxIn #rx((+ :digit)) (Some m)) (str "a number: " (rx-text m)))
      (_ "neither"))
    """)
  (reference """
    @code{Rx} is also the name of the type of a compiled regular expression,
    which @code{rx-compile} describes.

    A view covers nothing for exhaustiveness, so a @code{match} using
    @code{Rx} or @code{RxIn} still needs a clause that matches anything.

    The regular expression is evaluated each time the clause is tried. The
    compiled regex is cached, but a @code{#rx(...)} written in the clause is
    still looked up anew each time, so bind the pattern once, at the top
    level, and name it in the clause:

    @codeblock[#:lang "bjolang"]|{
    (def digits #rx((+ :digit)))

    (match text
      ((RxIn digits (Some m)) (rx-text m))
      (_ "none"))
    }|
    """))

(:doc RxIn
  (summary "A match pattern: searches the string for a regular expression.")
  (form (RxIn regex pattern))
  (arg regex "The regular expression: an @code{Rx}, such as a @code{#rx(...)} or a name bound to one.")
  (arg pattern "Matched against the @code{(Option RxMatch)} that @code{rx-search} answers.")
  (see Rx rx-search)
  (reference """
    As @code{Rx}, but finding the leftmost match anywhere in the string. A
    @code{match} using it still needs a clause that matches anything.
    """))
